  urldate = {2019-07-06},
  abstract = {OBJECTIVE. Recent publications showing unexpectedly early breast development in American girls created debate worldwide. However, secular trend analyses are often limited by poor data comparability among studies performed by different researchers in different time periods and populations. Here we present new European data systematically collected from the same region and by 1 research group at the www.pediatrics.org/cgi/doi/10.1542/ peds.2008-2491 doi:10.1542/peds.2008-2491 beginning and end of the recent 15-year period. Key Words puberty, breast development, menarche, METHODS. Girls (N ϭ 2095) aged 5.6 to 20.0 years were studied in 1991--1993 (1991 cohort; n ϭ 1100) and 2006 --2008 (2006 cohort; n ϭ 995). All girls were evaluated by palpation of glandular breast, measurement of height and weight, and blood sampling (for estradiol, luteinizing hormone, and follicle-stimulating hormone). Age distribution at entering pubertal breast stages 2 through 5, pubic hair stages 2 obesity, endocrine-disrupting chemicals, phytoestrogen Abbreviations Bn--- breast stage number PHn---pubic hair stage number FSH---follicle-stimulating hormone through 5, and menarche was estimated for the 2 cohorts. LH---luteinizing hormone PI---prediction interval RESULTS. Onset of puberty, defined as mean estimated age at attainment of glandular breast tissue (Tanner breast stage 2ϩ), occurred significantly earlier in the 2006 cohort (estimated mean age: 9.86 years) when compared with the 1991 cohort (estimated mean age: 10.88 years). The difference remained significant after adjustment for BMI. Estimated ages at menarche were 13.42 and 13.13 years in the 1991 CI--- confidence interval EDC--- endocrine-disrupting chemicals BPA--- bisphenol A Accepted for publication Feb 10, 2009 Address correspondence to Lise Aksglaede, MD, Rigshospitalet, Department of Growth and 2006 cohorts, respectively. Serum follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone did not differ between the 2 cohorts at any age interval, whereas significantly lower estradiol levels were found in 8- to 10-year-old girls from the 2006 cohort compared with similarly aged girls from the 1991 cohort. and Reproduction GR, Section 5064, Blegdamsvej 9, DK-2100 Copenhagen \O, Denmark. E-mail lise.aksglaede@rh.hosp.dk PEDIATRICS (ISSN Numbers: Print, 0031-4005; Online, 1098-4275). Copyright \copyright{} 2009 by the American Academy of Pediatrics CONCLUSIONS. We found significantly earlier breast development among girls born more recently. Alterations in reproductive hormones and BMI did not explain these marked changes, which suggests that other factors yet to be identified may be involved. Pediatrics 2009;123: e932--e939},
  langid = {english},
  file = {/home/lc/snap/zotero-snap/common/Zotero/storage/6UJJLCPQ/Aksglaede et al. - 2009 - Recent Decline in Age at Breast Development The C.pdf}
}

