; Mulgan, R., Processes of accountability (1997) Australian Journal of Public Administration, 56 (1), pp. 25-36. , doi:10.1111/j.1467-8500.1997.tb01238.x; Nixon, J., Professional identity and the restructuring of higher education (1996) Studies in Higher Education, 21 (1), pp. 5-16. , doi:10.1080/03075079612331381417; Nixon, J., Regenerating professionalism within the academic workplace (1997) The end of the professions? The restructuring of professional work, pp. 86-103. , In: Broadbent J., Dietrich M., Roberts J., editors London, London,: Routledge; Nixon, J., Marks, A., Rowland, S., Walker, M., Towards a new academic professionalism: A manifesto of hope (2001) British Journal of Sociology of Education, 22 (2), pp. 227-244; Nixon, J., Ranson, S., Theorising 'agreement': The moral bases of the emergent professionalism within the 'new' management of education (1997) Discourse: Studies in the Cultural Politics of Education, 18 (2), pp. 197-214. , doi:10.1080/0159630970180203; Page, S., The web of managerial accountability: The impact of reinventing government (2006) Administration & Society, 38 (2), pp. 166-197. , doi:10.1177/0095399705285990; Piper, D.W., (1994) Are professors professional? The organisation of university examinations, , London, London,: Jessica Kingsley; Pollitt, C., (2003) The essential public manager, , London: Open University Press/McGraw-Hill; Power, M., (1997) The audit society: Rituals of verification, , New York, New York,: Oxford University Press; (2003) Learning from subject review, pp. 1993-2001. , Quality Assurance Agency for Higher Education (QAA), Gloucester, Gloucester,: QAA; (2009) Handbook for institutional audit: England and Northern Ireland, , QAA, Mansfield,: Linney Direct; Romzek, B.S., Dubnick, M.J., Accountability (2000) Defining public administration: Selections from the international encyclopedia of public policy and administration, , In: Shafritz J. M., editors Boulder, CO, Boulder, CO,: Westview Press; Salter, B., Tapper, T., (1994) The state and higher education, , London, London,: Woburn Press; Scott, P., (1995) The meaning of mass higher education, , Buckingham, UK, Buckingham,: Open University Press; Sennett, R., (2008) The craftsman, , London, London,: Allen Lane; Shattock, M., (1994) The UGC and the management of British universities, , London, London,: SRHE & Open University Press; Shore, C., Wright, S., Coercive accountability: The rise of audit culture in higher education (2000) Audit cultures: Anthropological studies in accountability, ethics and the academy, pp. 57-89. , In: Strathern M., editors London, London,: Routledge; Sinclair, A., The chameleon of accountability: Forms and discourses (1995) Accounting, Organisations and Society, 20 (2-3), pp. 219-237. , doi:10.1016/0361-3682(93)E0003-Y; Taggart, G.J., A critical review of the role of the English funding body for higher education in the relationship between the state and higher education in the period 1945-2003 (Unpublished PhD thesis, University of Bristol) (2004); Thompson, D.F., Moral responsibility of public officials: The problem of many hands (1980) American Political Science Review, 74 (4), pp. 905-916. , doi:10.2307/1954312; Trow, M., Trust, markets and accountability in higher education: A comparative perspective (1996) Higher Education Policy, 9 (4), pp. 309-324. , doi:10.1016/S0952-8733(96)00029-3; Wilson, L., (1979) American academics: Then and now, , New York, New York,: Oxford University Press},
  abstract = {Declining trust in public services has led to increasing calls for higher education to be 'accountable' for the quality of its teaching and learning provision. However, increasing levels of quality evaluation have led academics to feel that their professionalism is under attack. Reflecting on this history and various dimensions of accountability, this paper seeks to interpret accountability by addressing two questions: What is accountability in higher education? How can it be related to academic professionalism? The paper argues that professionalism and accountability appear to be contradictory terms, but can exist simultaneously. If academics' moral and social responsibility becomes requisite to an enhanced social accounting for the quality of university teaching and learning, there could be less perceived tension between academic professionalism and accountability. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
