tions on investigating emotion in educational activity settings (2006) Educational Psychology Review, 18 (4), pp. 343-360. , doi:10.1007/s10648-006-9030-3; Sheets-Johnstone, M., Animation: The fundamental, essential and properly descriptive concept (2009) Continental Philosophy Review, 42 (3), pp. 375-400. , doi:10.1007/s11007-009-9109-x; Spencer-Oatey, H., Rapport management: A framework for analysis (2000) Culturally speaking: Managing rapport through talk across cultures, pp. 11-46. , London, London,: Continuum, In H. Spencer-Oatey (Ed.); Sutton, R.E., Wheatley, K.F., Teachers' emotions and teaching: A review of the literature and directions for future research (2003) Educational Psychology Review, 15 (4), pp. 327-358. , doi:10.1023/A:1026131715856; Thomson, P., Walker, M., (2010) Routledge doctoral student's companion: Getting to grips with research in education and the social sciences, , In: Thomson P., Walker M, editors London and New York, London and New York,: Routledge; Zembylas, M., The emotional characteristics of teaching: An ethnographic study of one teacher (2004) Teaching and Teacher Education, 20 (2), pp. 185-201. , doi:10.1016/j.tate.2003.09.008},
  abstract = {While the epistemological and ontological challenges faced by doctoral candidates are well documented, the same cannot be said of the emotional dimensions of the journey. This paper draws on Activity Theory in exploring the role of emotion in the longitudinal doctoral learning experiences of six international PhD candidates studying in Australia. Analysis reveals that writing and supervision practices are common sites of tension but that the prevailing culture of silence militates against systemic change. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
