nal of Educational Technology, 18 (1), pp. 40-56; Marks, G., McMillan, J., Australia: Changes in socioeconomic inequalities in university participation (2007) Stratification in higher education: A comparative study, pp. 351-373. , In: Shavit Y., Arum R., Gamoran A., editors Stanford, CA, Stanford, CA,: Stanford University Press; Moodie, G., Pathways and articulation into higher education (2010) In International Encyclopedia of Education, pp. 609-615. , Oxford, UK: Elsevier; Moore, T., Hough, B., (2005) The perils of skills: Towards a model of integrating graduate attributes into the disciplines, , Paper presented at the Language and Academic Skills Conference, Canberra; Muir, K., Katz, I., Purcal, C., Patulny, R., Flaxman, S., Abello, D., Hayes, A., (2009) National Evaluation (2004-2008) of the Stronger Families and Communities Strategy 2004-2009, , Canberra, Canberra,: Department of Families, Housing, Community Services and Indigenous Affairs; (2010) Australian Vocational Education and Training Statistics: Student outcomes, , National Centre for Vocational Education Research, Adelaide, South Australia, Adelaide, South Australia,: NCVER; Palmer, S., Holt, D., Farley, A., Towards new models of flexible education to enhance quality in Australian higher education (2010) Accessible elements: Teaching science online and at a distance, pp. 61-80. , In: Kennepohl D., Shaw L., editors Edmonton, AB, Edmonton, AB,: AU Press, Athabasca University; Pike, G., Kuh, G., First- and second-generation college students: A comparison of their engagement and intellectual development (2005) Journal of Higher Education, 76 (3), pp. 276-300. , doi:10.1353/jhe.2005.0021; Power, M., Gould-Morven, A., Head of gold, feet of clay: The online learning paradox (2011) International Review of Research in Open and Distance Learning, 12 (2), pp. 19-39; Russell, T., (2001) The no significant difference phenomenon: A comparative research annotated bibliography on technology for distance education, , Chicago, IL: International Distance Education Certification Center; Santiago, P., Tremblay, K., Basri, E., Arnal, E., (2008) Tertiary education for the knowledge society, , Paris, Paris,: OECD; Sitzmann, T., Brown, K., Casper, W., Ely, K., Zimmerman, R., A review and meta-analysis of the nomological network of trainee reactions (2008) Journal of Applied Psychology, 93 (2), pp. 280-295. , doi:10.1037/0021-9010.93.2.280; Sumsion, J., Goodfellow, J., Identifying generic skills through curriculum mapping: A critical evaluation (2004) Higher Education Research & Development, 23 (3), pp. 329-346. , doi:10.1080/0729436042000235436; (2009) TAFE - Higher Education articulation, 2003-2009, , http://www.swinburne.edu.au/corporate/spq/docs/swinonly/TAFEtoHEDarticulation2003_2003-2009.pdf, Swinburne University of Technology, Retrieved May 16, 2011, from; Tickell, G., Smyrnios, K., TAFE-to-university transition: The effectiveness of an accounting degree articulation program (2004) International Journal of Training Research, 2 (2), pp. 49-74. , doi:10.5172/ijtr.2.2.49; (2010) Dual-sector university cohesion: A discussion paper, , http://www.ballarat.edu.au/projects/dscp/docs/discuss.pdf, University of Ballarat & Swinburne University of Technology, Retrieved June 30, 2010, from; Wingate, U., Doing away with 'study skills (2006) Teaching in Higher Education, 11 (4), pp. 457-469. , doi:10.1080/13562510600874268; Woodley, C., Henderson, F., de Sensi, M., Gabb, R., (2005) Selection processes and pathways: TAFE to higher education, 2005, , Melbourne: Victoria University Press; Yorke, M., Longden, B., (2008) The first-year experience of higher education in the UK: Final report of a project funded by the Higher Education Academy, , York, UK, York,: Higher Education Academy},
  abstract = {Along with the massification of higher education comes a need for new models to support the success of greater numbers of diverse students. A greater proportion of these students are 'non-traditional' in terms of preparedness, socioeconomic status and geography. This paper introduces an Associate Degree model designed to support this new higher education reality of broader student cohorts, thin regional markets and cross-sectoral collaboration. Background literature on challenges facing the higher education sector and its prospective students is presented, with a particular focus on regionality. An argument is made for the role of curriculum and pedagogy as enablers of non-traditional student success. This is supported by the results of a mixed-methods exploratory study. This Associate Degree model was attractive to students and institutes. Students experienced similar levels of challenge, workload and progress to their traditional peers. While technology was essential for the success of the model, it played a supporting role to the relationships and multiple modes of learning it facilitated. This article provides insights for institutions seeking to address the broadening participation agenda. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,associate degree,diversity,participation,pathway,transition}
}

