uggia, M., Implications of the situated learning model for teaching and learning in nursing research (2000) Journal of Continuing Education in Nursing, 31 (6), pp. 263-268; Goold, S., Usher, K., Meeting the health needs of Indigenous people: How is nursing education meeting the challenge? (2006) Contemporary Nurse, 22 (2), pp. 288-295. , doi:10.5172/conu.2006.22.2.288; Handley, K., Sturdy, A., Fincham, R., Clark, T., Within and beyond communities of practice: Making sense of learning through participation, identity and practice (2006) Journal of Management Studies, 43 (3), pp. 641-653. , doi:10.1111/j.1467-6486.2006.00605.x; Hill, M., Augoustinos, M., Stereotype change and prejudice reduction: Short- and long-term evaluation of a cross cultural awareness program (2001) Journal of Community and Applied Social Psychology, 11 (4), pp. 243-262. , doi:10.1002/casp.629; Johnstone, M.J., Kanitsaki, O., The spectrum of 'new racism' and discrimination in hospital contexts (2009) Collegian, 16 (2), pp. 63-69. , doi:10.1016/j.colegn.2009.03.001; Kanter, S., Wimmers, P., Levine, A., In-depth learning: One school's initiatives to foster integration of ethics, values and the human dimensions of medicine (2007) Academic Medicine, 82 (4), pp. 405-409. , doi:10.1097/ACM.0b013e318033373c; Lave, J., Wenger, E., (2009) Situated learning: Legitimate peripheral participation, , Cambridge, Cambridge,: Cambridge University Press; Lin, I., Forrest, S.H.M., Scott, K., Taylor, K., Jones, S., (2010) Wiluna bush camp for staff: Institutional and curricula change through experiential remote and Indigenous health education in the bush, , April 6-9, Paper presented at the All Together Better Health 5: International Interprofessional Conference, Sydney, Australia; Lindquist, I., Engardt, M., Garnham, L., Poland, F., Richardson, B., Development pathways in learning to be a physiotherapist (2006) Physiotherapy Research International, 11 (3), pp. 129-139. , doi:10.1002/pri.332; McAllister, G., Irvine, J., Cross-cultural competency and multicultural teacher education (2000) Review of Educational Research, 70 (1), pp. 3-24. , doi:10.3102/00346543070001003; McLellan, H., (1996) Situated learning perspectives, , Englewood Cliffs, NJ, Englewood Cliffs, NJ,: Educational Technology Publications; Moreton Robinson, A., (2009) Talkin' up to the white woman, , Brisbane, Brisbane,: University of Queensland Press; Myers, S., Life skills training through situated learning experiences: An alternative instruction model (2011) International Journal of Special Education, 26 (3), pp. 1-8; O'Toole, K., Schoo, A., Stagnitti, K., Cuss, K., Rethinking policies for the retention of allied health professionals in rural areas: A social relations approach (2008) Health Policy, 87 (3), pp. 326-332. , doi:10.1016/j.healthpol.2008.01.012; Playford, D., Larson, A., Wheatland, B., Going country: Rural student placement factors associated with future rural employment in nursing and allied health (2006) Australian Journal of Rural Health, 14 (1), pp. 14-19. , doi:10.1111/j.1440-1584.2006.00745.x; Taylor, J., Blue, I., Misan, G., Approach to sustainable primary healthcare service delivery for rural and remote South Australia (2001) Australian Journal of Rural Health, 9 (6), pp. 304-310. , doi:10.1046/j.1038-5282.2001.00387.x; Taylor, K., Thompson, S.C., Smith, J., Dimer, L., Ali, M., Wood, M., Exploring the impact of an Aboriginal health worker on hospitalised Aboriginal experiences: Lessons from cardiology (2009) Australian Health Review, 33 (4), pp. 549-557. , doi:10.1071/AH090549; Thompson, S.C., Shahid, S., Bessarab, D., Durey, A., Davidson, P.M., Not just bricks and mortar: Planning hospital cancer services for Aboriginal people (2011) BMC Research Notes, 4 (62), pp. 1-9; Tindale, N., (1974) Western Australia: Tindale tribal boundaries, , http://www.dia.wa.gov.au/Documents/Maps/Maps%20June10/Tindale.pdf, Retrieved August 17, 2010, from; Tuhiwai Smith, L., (1999) Decolonising methodologies: Research and indigenous peoples, , London, London,: Zed Books},
  abstract = {Rural and Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander (Indigenous) health content in undergraduate health science curricula in Western Australia has been limited. In 2008, a three-and-a-half-day, rurally-based, intercultural and inter-disciplinary programme for academics from three universities aimed to improve how academics prepared health science students for work in this area. Situated learning theory underpinned the programme's design, which prioritised context and participation in the construction of knowledge: academics lived 'on country' and participated in the lived experience of a rural and Indigenous community. Semi-structured phone interviews with 21 academics four months later indicated this approach had radically changed thinking and led to a desire to improve rural and Indigenous health and teaching practice. Targeting academics to learn about rural and Indigenous health in situ is one promising strategy for improving undergraduate health science education in this priority area. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,health education,Indigenous health,racism,remote,rural,situated learning,teaching practice}
}

