.1080/02680930110071057; Pelias, R., (2004) A methodology of the heart: Evoking academic and daily life, , Walnut Creek, CA, Walnut Creek, CA,: AltaMira; Petersen, E.B., Staying or going? Australian early career researchers' narratives of academic work, exit options and coping strategies (2011) Australian Universities' Review, 53 (2), pp. 34-42; Phipps, A., The sound of higher education: Sensuous epistemologies and the mess of knowing (2007) London Review of Education, 5 (1), pp. 1-13. , doi:10.1080/14748460601045671; Piirto, J., The question of quality and qualifications: Writing inferior poems as qualitative research (2002) Qualitative Studies in Education, 15 (4), pp. 431-445. , doi:10.1080/09518390210145507; Poindexter, C.C., Research as poetry: A couple experiences HIV (2002) Qualitative Inquiry, 8 (6), pp. 707-714. , doi:10.1177/1077800402238075; Reay, D., Beyond consciousness? The psychic landscape of social class (2005) Sociology, 39 (5), pp. 911-928. , doi:10.1177/0038038505058372; Richardson, L., Poetics, dramatics and the transgressive validity: The case of the skipped line (1993) The Sociological Quarterly, 34 (4), pp. 695-710; Richardson, L., Nine poems: Marriage and the family (1994) Journal of Contemporary Ethnography, 23 (1), pp. 3-13. , doi:10.1177/089124194023001001; Richardson, L., (1997) Fields of play: Constructing an academic life, , New Brunswick, NJ, New Brunswick, NJ,: Rutgers University Press; Richardson, L., Skirting a pleated text: De-disciplining an academic life (1997) Qualitative Inquiry, 3 (3), pp. 295-303. , doi:10.1177/107780049700300303; Richardson, L., Writing as a method of inquiry (2000) Handbook of qualitative inquiry, pp. 923-948. , In: Denzin N. K., Lincoln Y., editors 2, London, London,: Sage; Richardson, L., Poetic representation of interviews (2002) Handbook of interview research, pp. 877-891. , In: Gubrium J., Holstein J., editors London, London,: Sage; Richardson, L., Writing sociology (2002) Cultural Studies-Critical Methodologies, 2 (3), pp. 414-422; Richardson, L., Sticks and stones: An exploration of the embodiment of social classism (2005) Qualitative Inquiry, 11 (4), pp. 485-491. , doi:10.1177/1077800405276807; Richardson, L., Lockridge, E., (2004) Travels with Ernest: Crossing the literary/sociological divide, , Walnut Creek, CA, Walnut Creek, CA,: Altamira; Shore, C., Audit culture and illiberal governance: Universities and the politics of accountability (2008) Anthropological Theory, 8 (3), pp. 278-298. , doi:10.1177/1463499608093815; Shore, C., Wright, S., Whose accountability: Governmentality and the auditing of universities (2004) Parallax, 10 (2), pp. 100-116. , doi:10.1080/1353464042000208558; Sparkes, A., Embodiment, academics, and the audit culture: A story seeking consideration (2007) Qualitative Research, 7 (4), pp. 521-550. , doi:10.1177/1468794107082306; Sparkes, A., Douglas, K., Making a case for poetic representations: An example in action (2007) Sport Psychologist, 21 (2), pp. 170-190; Sparkes, A., Nilges, L., Swan, P., Dowling, F., Poetic representations in sport and physical education: Insider perspectives (2003) Sport, Education and Society, 8 (2), pp. 153-177. , doi:10.1080/13573320309256; Strathern, M., The tyranny of transparency (2000) British Educational Research Journal, 26 (3), pp. 309-321. , doi:10.1080/713651562; Williams, C.W., (1917) 'January Morning', , http://www.theotherpages.org/poems/2001/williams0102.html, Retrieved March 19, 2012, from},
  abstract = {Five poetic transcriptions lie at the heart of this article. We intend that they will convey something of what it means and feels like to be an academic researcher in neoliberal universities such that we begin to notice this condition and its effects more acutely. Our poetic texts began their existence as fast-written prose responses to the question: 'If you say to yourself, "I am a researcher", how do you feel, what do you think about and what associations do you make?' In this article, we foreground an exploration of the poetic mode of representing research data through example and exegesis. We argue this kind of interpretive work is effective because it strikes a different kind of relationship between empirical data and reader, one that encourages 'reverberation' - a resounding way of noticing that draws upon our bodily and emotional reactions to a text as well as our intellectual ones. Hence, a methodology of poetic transcriptions may be especially capable of evoking for the reader some of the problematic, although variable, effects of the Anglo-Western neoliberal - or 'managerialist' - university on the hearts and minds of its beleaguered academic subjects. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,academic researchers,emotions,managerialism,poetic transcriptions,research methodologies}
}

