.1080/01580370701424650; Manathunga, C., Supervision as a contested space: A response (2009) Teaching in Higher Education, 14 (3), pp. 341-345. , doi:10.1080/13562510902990242; Manderson, D., Asking better questions: Approaching the process of thesis supervision (1996) Journal of Legal Education, 46 (3), pp. 407-419; Marton, F., Booth, S., (1997) Learning and awareness, , Mahwah, NJ, Mahwah, NJ,: Lawrence Erlbaum & Associates; McInnes, C., Changing academic work roles: The everyday realities challenging quality in teaching (2000) Quality in Higher Education, 6 (2), pp. 143-152. , doi:10.1080/713692738; Middleton, S., Rhythms of place: Time and space in the doctoral experience (2010) The Routledge doctoral supervisor's companion: Supporting effective research in education and the social sciences, pp. 185-196. , In: Walker M., Thompson P., editors London, London,: Routledge; Misztal, B.A., (2000) Informality: Social theory and contemporary practice, , London, London,: Routledge; Misztal, B.A., The new importance of the relationship between formality and informality (2005) Feminist Theory, 6 (2), pp. 173-194. , doi:10.1177/1464700105053693; Montgomery, J., Café culture and the city: The role of pavement cafés in urban public social life (1997) Journal of Urban Design, 2 (1), pp. 83-102. , doi:10.1080/13574809708724397; Murphy, N., Research supervision: Matches and mismatches (2009) International Journal of Electrical Engineering Education, 46 (3), pp. 295-306; Murphy, N., Bain, J.D., Conrad, L., Orientations to research higher degree supervision (2007) Higher Education, 53 (2), pp. 209-234. , doi:10.1007/s10734-005-5608-9; Oldenburg, R., (1989) The great good place: Cafés, coffee shops, community centres, beauty parlours, general stores, bars, hangouts and how they get you through the day, , New York, New York,: Paragon House; Oldenburg, R., Brissett, D., The third place (1982) Qualitative Sociology, 5 (4), pp. 265-284. , doi:10.1007/BF00986754; Schensul, S., Schensul, J., LeCompte, M., (1999) Essential ethnographic methods, , Walnut Creek, CA, Walnut Creek, CA,: AltaMira Press; Scheyvens, R., Wild, K., Overton, J., International students pursuing postgraduate study in geography: Impediments to their learning experiences (2003) Journal of Geography in Higher Education, 27 (3), pp. 309-323. , doi:10.1080/0309826032000145070; Sinclair, M., (2004) The pedagogy of 'good' PhD supervision: A national cross-disciplinary investigation of PhD supervision, , Canberra, Canberra,: Department of Education, Science and Training; Taylor, S., Beasley, N., (2005) A handbook for doctoral supervisors, , London, London,: Routledge; Wisker, G., Robinson, G., Shacham, M., Postgraduate research success: Communities of practice involving cohorts, guardian supervisors and online communities (2007) Innovations in Education and Teaching International, 44 (3), pp. 301-320. , doi:10.1080/14703290701486720; Wright, A., Murray, J.P., Geale, P., A phenomenographic study of what it means to supervise doctoral students (2007) Academy of Management Learning and Education, 6 (4), pp. 458-474. , doi:10.5465/AMLE.2007.27694946; Yeatman, A., Making supervisory relationships accountable: Graduate student logs (1995) Australian Universities Review, 38 (2), pp. 9-11},
  abstract = {A great deal of literature in recent years has focused on the supervisory relationship, yet very little has been written about the nature or content of supervisory meetings, beyond commenting on the frequency and length of meetings. Through semi-structured interviews, informal discussions with colleagues and students, a critical review of literature and personal reflection, this paper explores the salience of coffee for postgraduate supervision. This paper locates supervisions over coffee in reference to contemporary debates about the supervisory relationship (models, styles, tasks and dimensions). Using the concept of 'third places' and Misztal's theorisation of informality, it is argued that supervision over coffee conveys a particular supervisory relationship to postgraduate students: one that is incompatible with expert-disciple models or styles of supervision. Instead, supervision over coffee is on neutral territory and on a more informal footing. Finally the paper concludes with discussion about finding a balance between formality and informality in supervision and the development of personal and institutional trust. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,formality,higher degree supervision,supervisory meetings,supervisory relationships,third places}
}

