y and bureaucratic (2011) The Australian, , http://www.theaustralian.com.au/higher-education/era-set-up-clumsy-and-bureaucratic/story-e6frgcjx-1226045192091, Retrieved April 8, 2013, from; Hoddell, S., Street, D., Wildblood, H., Doctorates: Converging or diverging patterns of provision (2002) Quality Assurance in Education, 10 (2), pp. 61-70. , doi:10.1108/09684880210423546; Jackson, C., Tinkler, P., Back to basics: A consideration of the purposes of the PhD viva (2001) Assessment & Evaluation in Higher Education, 26 (4), pp. 355-366. , doi:10.1080/02602930120063501; Kamler, B., Rethinking doctoral publication practices: Writing from and beyond the thesis (2008) Studies in Higher Education, 33 (3), pp. 283-294. , doi:10.1080/03075070802049236; Lee, A., Developing effective supervisors: Concepts of research supervision (2007) South African Journal of Higher Education, 21 (4), pp. 680-693; Lee, A., When the article is the dissertation: Pedagogies for a PhD by publication (2010) Publishing pedagogies for the Doctorate and beyond, pp. 12-29. , In: Aitchison C., Kamler B., Lee A., editors Hoboken, NJ, Hoboken, NJ,: Routledge; Lee, A., Kamler, B., Bringing pedagogy to doctoral publishing (2008) Teaching in Higher Education, 13 (5), pp. 511-523. , doi:10.1080/13562510802334723; McGrail, M., Rickard, C., Jones, R., Publish or perish: A systematic review of interventions to increase academic publication rates (2006) Higher Education Research & Development, 25 (1), pp. 19-35. , doi:10.1080/07294360500453053; Mowbray, S., Halse, C., The purpose of the PhD: Theorising the skills acquired by students (2010) Higher Education Research & Development, 29 (6), pp. 653-664. , doi:10.1080/07294360.2010.487199; Paré, A., Slow the presses: Concerns about premature publication (2010) Publishing pedagogies for the Doctorate and beyond, pp. 30-46. , In: Aithchison C., Kamler B., Lee A., editors New York, New York,: Routledge; Park, C., New variant PhD: The changing nature of the Doctorate in the UK (2005) Journal of Higher Education Policy and Management, 27 (2), pp. 189-207. , doi:10.1080/13600800500120068; Park, C., (2007) Redefining the Doctorate, , London, London,: Higher Education Academy; Peters, M., (2007) Knowledge economy, development and the future of higher education, , Rotterdam, The Netherlands, Rotterdam,: Sense; Powell, S., (2004) The award of the PhD by published work, , Lichfield, UK, Lichfield,: Council for Graduate Education; Powell, S., Green, H., (2007) The doctorate worldwide, , Buckingham, UK, Buckingham,: Open University Press; Robins, L., Kanowski, P., PhD by publication: A student's perspective (2008) Journal of Research Practice, 4 (2). , http://jrp.icaap.org/index.php/jrp/article/view/136/154, Article M3. Retrieved April 8, 2013, from; Rowbotham, J., Students want a career in academe (2011) The Australian, , http://www.theaustralian.com.au/higher-education/students-want-a-career-in-academe/story-e6frgcjx-1226045180536, Retrieved April 8, 2013, from; Simpson, R., (1983) How the PhD came to Britain: A century of struggle for postgraduate education, , Guildford, UK, Guildford,: Society for Research in Higher Education; Starrs, B., Publish and graduate? Earning a PhD by published papers in Australia (2008) M/C: A Journal of Media and Culture, 11 (4). , http://journal.media-culture.org.au/index.php/mcjournal/article/viewArticle/37I, Retrieved January 11, 2011, from; Tight, M., Are academic workloads increasing? The post-war survey evidence in the UK (2010) Higher Education Quarterly, 64 (2), pp. 200-215. , doi:10.1111/j.1468-2273.2009.00433.x; Timmons, S., Park, J., A qualitative study of the factors influencing the submission for publication of research undertaken by students (2008) Nurse Education Today, 28 (6), pp. 744-750. , doi:10.1016/j.nedt.2007.12.002; (1998) The status of published work in submissions for Doctoral degrees in European universities, , UK Council for Graduate Education (UKCGE), London, London,: Author; Ware, M., Monkman, M., (2008) Peer review in scholarly journals: Perspective of the scholarly community: An international study, , London, UK,: Publishing Research Consortium; Webb, C., (2008) Writing for publication, , http://www.nurseauthoreditor.com/WritingforPublicationbooklet2008.pdf, Retrieved April 8, 2013, from; Wilson, K., Quality assurance issues for a PhD by published work: A case study (2002) Quality Assurance in Education, 10 (2), pp. 71-78. , doi:10.1108/09684880210423555},
  abstract = {There is increasing impetus for higher-degree-by-research students to publish during candidature. Research performance, including higher degree completions and publication output, commonly determines university funding, and doctorates with publishing experience are better positioned for a career in softening academic labour markets. The PhD by Publication provides a pathway for candidates to foster and demonstrate their publishing capabilities. It also provides existing academics a means of achieving doctoral status while managing the 'publish or perish' milieu endemic to their work. This paper clarifies the precise nature and significance of the PhD by Publication pathway in the Australian context and discusses the associated benefits and problems, enriched by personal experience. It summarises factors pertinent to assessing the pathway's suitability. The review of current policy suggests institutional guidelines in universities nationwide are inadequate for producing theses of comparable quality to conventional dissertations and capitalising on the pathway's significant benefits. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
