  abstract = {The two case studies presented explore the potential offered by in-depth qualitative analysis of students\^a\texteuro ™ online discussion to enhance our understanding of how students learn. Both cases are used to illustrate how the monitoring and moderation of online student group communication can open up a \^a\texteuro\texttildelow window into learning\^a\texteuro ™, providing us with new insights into complex problem-solving and thinking processes. The cases offer examples of students\^a\texteuro ™ \^a\texteuro\texttildelow thinking aloud\^a\texteuro ™ while problem-solving, showing how and why they arrived at particular outcomes and the underlying thought processes involved. It is argued that these insights into students\^a\texteuro ™ learning processes can in turn offer us the opportunity to adapt our own teaching practice in order to achieve a better pedagogical \^a\texteuro\texttildelow fit\^a\texteuro ™ with the learning needs of our students; for example, through a more precise or more timely intervention. It is also suggested that looking through this \^a\texteuro\texttildelow window\^a\texteuro ™ enables us to concentrate our assessment more closely on the \emph{process} of task completion, rather than focusing solely on the end \emph{product}.},
  keywords = {/unread}
}

