) Desire in language: A semiotic approach to literature and art, , Oxford, Oxford,: Basil Blackwell; Lovitts, B., (2007) Making the implicit explicit: Creating performance expectations for the dissertation, , Sterling, VA, Sterling, VA,: Stylus; McGann, J., (1991) The textual condition, , Princeton, NJ, Princeton, NJ,: Princeton University Press; Morrison, T., (1993) Playing in the dark: Whiteness and the literary imagination, , London, London,: Pan Macmillan; Murdoch, I., Against dryness: A polemical sketch (1961) Encounter, 16, pp. 16-20; Nussbaum, M., (1997) Cultivating humanity: A classical defense of reform in liberal education, , Cambridge, MA, Cambridge, MA,: Harvard University Press; Richardson, L., Writing: A method of inquiry (2005) Handbook of qualitative research, pp. 959-978. , In: Denzin N. K., Lincoln Y. S., editors Thousand Oaks, CA, Thousand Oaks, CA,: Sage; Saltmarsh, S., Economic subjectivities in higher education: Self, policy and practice in the knowledge economy (2011) Cultural Studies Review, 17 (2), pp. 115-139; Shore, C., Wright, S., Audit culture and anthropology: Neo-liberalism in British higher education (1999) Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute, 5 (4), pp. 557-575. , doi:10.2307/2661148; Smiles, S., (1859) Self-help: With illustrations of conduct and perseverance. EBSCO Publishing, , http://web.ebscohost.com.ezproxy.auckland.ac.nz/ehost/ebookviewer/ebook/nlebk_1039600_AN?sid=72524a4e-f90a-4b7d-a6c7-040eab7370c3@sessionmgr12&vid=1, Retrieved May 5, 2012; Thomson, P., Walker, M., Doctoral education in context: The changing nature of the doctorate and doctoral students (2010) The Routledge doctoral supervisor's companion: Supporting effective research in education and the social sciences, pp. 9-26. , In: Walker M., Thomson P., editors Abingdon, UK, Abingdon, UK,: Routledge; Tierney, W.G., Life history's history: Subjects foretold (1998) Qualitative Inquiry, 4 (1), pp. 49-70; Tosh, J., Men and domesticity (2007) The Victorian studies reader, pp. 318-325. , In: Boyd K., McWilliam R., editors London, London,: Routledge; Ylijoki, O.H., Master's thesis writing from a narrative approach (2001) Studies in Higher Education, 26 (1), pp. 21-34. , doi:10.1080/03075070020030698; Ylijoki, O.H., Academic nostalgia: A narrative approach to academic work (2005) Human Relations, 58 (5), pp. 555-576. , doi:10.1177/0018726705055963},
  abstract = {This paper looks at ways in which methods used in literary analysis can contribute to higher education research. I start by describing a methodology commonly used in the analysis of literary texts, close reading or textual analysis, and outline how it can be utilised in conjunction with several theoretical approaches in the literary studies toolkit. I then demonstrate how these methodological approaches might work in higher education research through a brief analysis of two texts produced by two universities, focusing on the construction of the doctoral researcher in each. Finally, I close by briefly considering what kinds of texts could be analysed in a higher education research context and argue for a broadening of what counts as data in the field. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,close analysis,doctoral education,literary theory,research methodologies}
}

@article{kellyDayLifeDeath2015,
  title = {A Day in the Life (and Death) of a Public University},
  author = {Kelly, F.},
  year = 2015,
  journal = {Higher Education Research and Development},
  volume = {34},
  number = {6},
  pages = {1153--1163},
  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/07294360.2015.1024628},
  abstract = {This is a narrative of an actual day in the author's working life at a large public university in the southern hemisphere. It is an enquiry into life, and death, at the university. It attempts to balance a critical and informed perspective with a lived perspective and, as a story that contributes to a developing genre of academic writing, it works to counter the dominant neo-liberal discourse in the university by reaffirming the value of the imagination. It reflects on a remarkable--unremarkable day to show the complexity of being an individual subject, situated in language, hailed by different discourses, feeling and sometimes thinking contradictory things at once, in a contemporary university context. \copyright{} 2015 HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,academic work,discourse,narrative,space,subjectivity,university}
}

@article{kellyMethodsAssessingHigher2020,
  title = {Methods for Assessing Higher Education Research Team Collaboration: Comparing Research Outputs and Participant Perceptions across Four Collaborative Research Teams},
  author = {Kelly, N. and Doyle, J. and Parker, M.},
  year = 2020,
  journal = {Higher Education Research and Development},
  volume = {39},
  number = {2},
  pages = {215--229},
  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/07294360.2019.1676199},
  abstract = {This article suggests that situations in which multiple research teams are convened under similar conditions present an opportunity to discover factors that lead to productive collaboration. It argues that social network analysis of research team outputs becomes more valuable when paired with data about research participant perceptions; and that any variables used as indicators of collaboration need to be calibrated using datasets from multiple studies with cross-team comparisons. The article provides an example of the kind of methodology needed to achieve this, describing a study with data from four research teams based at an Australian university campus, reporting their research performance over four years under conditions in which many variables were controlled and with results augmented by a survey of participant perceptions. Findings from the study indicate that there were exceptions to hypothesized associations between participant perceptions of collaboration and specific social network analysis measures over co-authorship data. The article suggests that, given the methodological challenges of studying research teams in the field, multiple datasets combining findings such as those in the present study are a path towards the development of indicators of productive higher education research collaboration. \copyright{} 2019, \copyright{} 2019 HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,Australia,bibliometric,research collaboration,research evaluation,Research policy,science of team science,social network analysis},
  file = {/home/lc/snap/zotero-snap/common/Zotero/storage/RGCF7CEC/Kelly et al. - 2020 - Methods for assessing higher education research team collaboration comparing research outputs and p.pdf}
}

