ance teaching and learning: Ideas and approaches for work-related and placement learning, , https://www.reading.ac.uk/web/FILES/ccms/B02056_Employment_Engagment_AS_V6.pdf, Reading, UK: Centre for Career Management Skills, Retrieved March 3, 2015, from; Stiwne, E.E., Jungert, T., Engineering students’ experiences of transition from study to work (2010) Journal of Education and Work, 23 (5), pp. 417-437; Su, Y., Self-directed, genuine graduate attributes: The person-based approach (2014) Higher Education Research & Development, 33 (6), pp. 1208-1220; Taylor, A.R., Hooley, T., Evaluating the impact of career management skills module and internships programme within a university business school (2014) British Journal of Guidance & Counselling, 42 (5), pp. 487-499; Taylor, A.R., Raykov, M., Hamm, Z., Exploring outcomes of youth apprenticeship in Canada (2014) Understanding employer engagement in education: Theories and evidence, pp. 221-235. , Mann A., Stanley J., Archer L., (eds), Abingdon, Oxon: Routledge; Thomas, K., Wong, K., Li, Y., The capstone experience: Student and academic perspectives (2014) Higher Education Research & Development, 33 (3), pp. 580-594; Try, S., The use of job search strategies among university graduates (2005) Journal of Socio-Economics, 34 (2), p. 223; Venables, A., Tan, G., Realizing learning in the workplace in an undergraduate IT program (2009) Journal of Information Technology Education: Innovations in practice, 8, pp. 17-26; Walkington, H., (2014), April, Enhancing the STEM student journey: Students as researchers. Keynote presentation to the HEA STEM Annual Learning and Teaching Conference 2014, Edinburgh, Scotland; Watanabe, M., Networking, networking, networking, networking, networking (2004) Nature, 430 (7001), pp. 812-813; Watson, R., A rationale for the development of an extracurricular employability award at a British university (2011) Research in Post-Compulsory Education, 16 (3), pp. 371-384; Yorke, M., (2006), https://www.heacademy.ac.uk/node/3263, Employability in higher education: What it is – what it is not. Retrieved January 11, 2015, from; Yorke, M., Employability: Aligning the message, the medium and academic values (2010) Journal of Teaching and Learning for Graduate Employability, 1 (1), pp. 2-12; Yorke, M., Knight, P., Curricula for economic and social gain (2006) Higher Education, 51 (4), pp. 565-588},
  abstract = {A literature review identified 12 strategies that have been empirically linked to improvements in graduate employability. A survey methodology was used to investigate self-reported use and/or perspectives on these strategies among four stakeholder groups. The following questions were asked: to students -- What strategies are you using to improve your graduate employability; to graduates -- What strategies did you use to improve your employability?; to higher education career development professionals and educators -- Which of the following employability strategies do you provide for students?; and to employers -- Which of the following strategies undertaken by students does your organisation value when recruiting graduates? Across the four stakeholder groups, 705 responses were received and analysed. The key findings were discrepancies between the strategies reported in the literature and those indicated in the surveys, as well as discrepancies between stakeholder groups in regard to which strategies were indicated. \copyright{} 2016 HERDSA.},
