  abstract = {Internship programs, whereby students are employed on a full- time basis while studying part-time for a degree, are increasingly popular. This paper discusses an analytical framework which is helpful in evaluating, from the student's perspective, alternative internship programs. The methodology is illustrated by a detailed analysis of two internship programs currently available in N.S.W. Other options such as full-time study, with and without part-time employment, are also analysed. Comparison of the internships and other options reveal substantial differences in life-time earnings, rates of renumeration and implicit trade-offs between income and leisure. Non-monetary differences may also be substantial. \copyright{} 1987, Taylor \& Francis Group, LLC. All rights reserved.},
