dle River, NJ, Upper Saddle River, NJ,: Merrill Printice Hall; Pintrich, P.R., Smith, D.A.F., Garcia, T., McKeachie, W.J., Reliability and predictive validity of the Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire (MSLQ) (1993) Educational and Psychological Measurement, 53 (3), pp. 801-813; Pintrich, P.R., Zusho, A., Student motivation and self-regulated learning in college classroom (2007) The scholarship of teaching and learning in higher education: Evidence-based perspective, pp. 731-810. , In: Perry R. P., Smart J. C., editors New York, New York,: Springer; Pokay, P., Blumenfeld, P.C., Predicting achievement in early and late in the semester: The role of motivation and use of learning strategies (1990) Journal of Educational Psychology, 82 (1), pp. 41-50; Ramsden, P., A performance indicator of teaching quality in higher education: The Course Experience Questionnaire (1991) Studies in Higher Education, 16 (2), pp. 129-150; Richardson, J.T.E., Approaches to studying and perceptions of academic quality in a short web-based course (2003) British Journal of Educational Technology, 34 (4), pp. 433-442; Salili, F., Learning and motivation: An Asian perspective (1996) Psychology in Developing Societies, 8 (1), pp. 55-81; Schunk, D.H., Effects of effort attributional feedback on children's perceived self-efficacy and achievement (1982) Journal of Educational Psychology, 74 (4), pp. 548-556; Schunk, D.H., Sef-efficacy and educational instruction (1995) Self-efficy, adaptation and adjustment: Theory, research and application, pp. 281-303. , In: Maddux J. E., editors New York: Plenum Press; Schunk, D.H., Pintrich, P.R., Meece, J.L., (2008) Motivation in education: Theory, research and applications, , 3, Englewood Cliffs, NJ, Englewood Cliffs, NJ,: Prentice Hall; Skinner, E., Zimmere-Gembeck, M.J., Connel, J.P., Individual differences and the development of perceived control (1998) Monographs of the Society for Research in Child Development, 63 (2-3), pp. 1-234; Tinto, V., (1987) Leaving college: Rethinking the causes and cures of student attrition, , Chicago, IL, Chicago, IL,: The University of Chicago Press; Trigwell, K., Prosser, M., Improving the quality of learning: The influence of learning context and student approaches to learning on learning outcomes (1991) Higher Education, 22 (3), pp. 251-266; (1998) World declaration on higher education for the twenty-first century:Vision and action and framework for priority action for change and development in higher education adopted by the World Conference on Higher Education Higher Education in the Twenty-First Century: Vision and action, , UNESCO, Paris,: Author; Watson, M., McSorley, M., Foxcroft, C., Watson, A., Exploring the motivational orientation and learning strategies of first year university learners (2004) Tertiary Education and Management, 10 (3), pp. 193-207; Wigfield, A., Eccles, J.S., Expectancy-value theory of achievement motivation (2000) Contemporary Educational Psychology, 25 (1), pp. 68-81; Wilson, K.L., Lizzio, A., Ramsden, P., The development, validation and application of the Course Experience Questionnaire (1997) Studies in Higher Education, 22 (1), pp. 33-53},
  abstract = {While there is considerable literature concerning the relationship between student motivation and achievement, few studies have examined the relationship between student motivation, course experiences and academic achievement at the postgraduate level. This paper is based on the findings of the initial correlation analysis of the relationship between motivational beliefs and course experiences of 368 postgraduate students in five different subject areas enrolled in day and afternoon classes at a highly-ranked public university in Pakistan. A questionnaire was developed using scales from the Motivated Strategies for Learning Questionnaire and the Course Experiences Questionnaire to measure the motivational beliefs and course experiences of students. Academic achievement was measured using the achievement score in the courses in which the data were collected. The results of the study indicated that students' achievement scores were positively correlated with their self-efficacy for learning and negatively correlated with test anxiety, whereas the course-experience factor learning community was significantly correlated with achievement scores. Significant correlations were also found among almost all motivational beliefs and course experience factors. \copyright{} 2013 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,academic achievement,course experiences,motivational beliefs}
}

@article{naviauxMetabolicFeaturesChronic2016,
  title = {Metabolic Features of Chronic Fatigue Syndrome},
  author = {Naviaux, Robert K. and Naviaux, Jane C. and Li, Kefeng and Bright, A. Taylor and Alaynick, William A. and Wang, Lin and Baxter, Asha and Nathan, Neil and Anderson, Wayne and Gordon, Eric},
  year = 2016,
  month = sep,
  journal = {Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences},
  volume = {113},
  number = {37},
  pages = {E5472-E5480},
  publisher = {Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences},
  doi = {10.1073/pnas.1607571113},
  urldate = {2026-01-14},
  abstract = {More than 2 million people in the United States have myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (ME/CFS). We performed targeted, broad-spectrum metabolomics to gain insights into the biology of CFS. We studied a total of 84 subjects using these methods. Forty-five subjects (n = 22 men and 23 women) met diagnostic criteria for ME/CFS by Institute of Medicine, Canadian, and Fukuda criteria. Thirty-nine subjects (n = 18 men and 21 women) were age- and sex-matched normal controls. Males with CFS were 53 (\textpm 2.8) y old (mean \textpm{} SEM; range, 21--67 y). Females were 52 (\textpm 2.5) y old (range, 20--67 y). The Karnofsky performance scores were 62 (\textpm 3.2) for males and 54 (\textpm 3.3) for females. We targeted 612 metabolites in plasma from 63 biochemical pathways by hydrophilic interaction liquid chromatography, electrospray ionization, and tandem mass spectrometry in a single-injection method. Patients with CFS showed abnormalities in 20 metabolic pathways. Eighty percent of the diagnostic metabolites were decreased, consistent with a hypometabolic syndrome. Pathway abnormalities included sphingolipid, phospholipid, purine, cholesterol, microbiome, pyrroline-5-carboxylate, riboflavin, branch chain amino acid, peroxisomal, and mitochondrial metabolism. Area under the receiver operator characteristic curve analysis showed diagnostic accuracies of 94\% [95\% confidence interval (CI), 84--100\%] in males using eight metabolites and 96\% (95\% CI, 86--100\%) in females using 13 metabolites. Our data show that despite the heterogeneity of factors leading to CFS, the cellular metabolic response in patients was homogeneous, statistically robust, and chemically similar to the evolutionarily conserved persistence response to environmental stress known as dauer.},
  file = {/home/lc/snap/zotero-snap/common/Zotero/storage/QN6MFSLZ/Naviaux et al. - 2016 - Metabolic features of chronic fatigue syndrome.pdf}
}

@article{naylorIdentifyingAttritionRisk2018,
  title = {Identifying Attrition Risk Based on the First Year Experience},
  author = {Naylor, R. and Baik, C. and Arkoudis, S.},
  year = 2018,
  journal = {Higher Education Research and Development},
  volume = {37},
  number = {2},
  pages = {328--342},
  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/07294360.2017.1370438},
  abstract = {Using data collected from a recent national survey of Australian first-year students, this paper defines and validates four scales--belonging, feeling supported, intellectual engagement and workload stress--to measure the student experience of university. These scales provide insights into the university experience for both groups and individual students, to assist institutions in supporting and managing successful outcomes for their students. These data highlight the particular importance of a sense of belonging and mitigating workload stress to addressing attrition risk. Many traditional equity groups were found to only differ significantly from `traditional' students on the stress scale. However, these groups potentially contain enormous individual variation, and even the average experiences of these groups reveal that complexity in the engagement, motivation and sense of belonging of students from equity backgrounds. Importantly, the scales presented in this paper can easily be used by practitioners, researchers and institutions to identify risk of attrition at an individual level based on attitudinal factors rather than background or behavioural factors. This may inform university strategy and practice to enhance the student experience and increase retention across the sector. \copyright{} 2017 HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,attrition,first year experience,retention,Student engagement}
}

@article{naylorStructuralInequalityFramework2020,
  ids = {naylorStructuralInequalityFramework2020a},
  title = {Towards a Structural Inequality Framework for Student Retention and Success},
  author = {Naylor, R. and Mifsud, N.},
  year = 2020,
  journal = {Higher Education Research and Development},
  volume = {39},
  number = {2},
  pages = {259--272},
  issn = {07294360 (ISSN)},
  doi = {10.1080/07294360.2019.1670143},
  abstract = {University campuses are increasingly diverse, reflecting substantial growth in student enrolments, but this has not translated to equitable outcomes for all students. While much attention has been focused on student retention and success, particularly for those from non-traditional backgrounds, dominant theoretical models rest on a limited notion of cultural capital that places undue responsibility on students themselves. We suggest that structural inequality, whereby some people receive unequal privileges and opportunities, offers a more productive, less problematic framework for use by academic staff, university leaders and policy makers to address these challenges. In this article, we identify three types of structural inequality--vertical, horizontal, and internal--and include a taxonomy of internal inequalities to prompt further research and policy outcomes. Put simply, rather than ask how students can build cultural capital to assimilate to their institutions, we should ask what institutions can do to include students, staff, and the wider community. \copyright{} 2019, \copyright{} 2019 HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,attrition,higher education,retention,Social inclusion,structural inequality}
}

