  abstract = {This article explores some of the basic shortcomings and fallacies of managerial behavior in dynamic situations. In a laboratory study, 20 groups of three participants each, all with an education in business management, were observed while vying to manage a computer-simulated industrial organization called MANUTEX. This is an interactive simulation of a small garment factory. For most groups, this problem proved to be extremely difficult, The analysts of the problem-solving,process and the strategies that the participants employ show that several typical mistakes were responsible for the groups' difficulties. These mistakes am integrated into a number of generic behavior patterns and uncontested basic assumptions that guide action. The participants' difficulties are not due to insufficient managerial knowledge or cognitive limitations. Rather they stem from an incorrect use of the available knowledge, a tendency to avoid risks and reduce uncertainty, and a motivational process directed at sheltering the subjective sense of competence.},
  keywords = {/unread,Computer simulation_Usage,Human resource management_Research,Management games_Usage,Management science_Research,Management_Methods,Methods,Problem solving_Research,Research,Usage}
}

