ion reforms, pp. 201-217. , In: Arnot M., Barton L., editors Wallingford,UK: Triangle; Delamont, S., (2002) Fieldwork in educational settings: Methods, pitfalls and procedures, 2. , London: Falmer; Deming, W.E., (1993) The new economics for industry, government, education, , Cambridge, MA: MIT Centre for Advanced Engineering Studies; Dimitriadis, G., Series editor's introduction (2009) Theory and educational research: Toward critical social explanation, pp. vii-vix. , In: Anyon J., editors New York and London: Routledge; Fine, M., Epilogue (2009) Theory and educational research: Toward critical social explanation, pp. 179-196. , In: Anyon J., editors New York and London: Routledge; Foucault, M., (1977) Language, counter-memory, practice: Selected interviews and essays, , (D. F. Bouchard, Ed.) New York: Cornell University Press; Giddens, A., (1984) The constitution of society, , Cambridge: Polity Press; Glaser, B.G., Strauss, A.L., (1967) The discovery of grounded theory, , Chicago: Aldine; Gouldner, A., (1970) The coming crisis of Western sociology, , New York: Avon; Hammersley, M., Troubling theory in case study research (2012) Higher Education Research & Development, 31 (3), pp. 393-405; Head, B., Alford, J., (2008) Wicked problems: The implications for public management, , Paper presented at the International Research Society for Public Management 12th Annual Conference, Brisbane, QLD; Hey, V., The politics of performative resignification: Translating Judith Butler's theoretical discourse and its potential for a sociology of education (2006) British Journal of Sociology of Education, 27 (4), pp. 439-457; Jansen, J., The challenge of ordinary (2008) Radicalizing educational leadership, pp. 147-156. , In: Bogotch I., Beachum F., Blount J., Brooks J., English F., Jansen J., editors Rotterdam: Sense; Lave, J., Wenger, E., (1991) Situated learning: Legitimate peripheral participation, , Cambridge: Cambridge University Press; Lukes, S., On the social determination of truth (1973) Modes of thought, pp. 138-153. , In: Horton R., Finnegan R., editors London: Faber; Merton, R., (1949) Social theory and social structure, , New York: Free Press; Mills, C.W., (1959) The sociological imagination, , Harmondsworth,UK: Penguin; Patton, Q.M., (1997) Utilization focused evaluation: The new century text, 3. , London: Sage; Popper, K., (1945) The open society and its enemies, , London: Routledge and Kegan Paul; Popper, K., (1957) The poverty of historicism, , London: Routledge and Kegan Paul; Popper, K., (1963) Conjectures and refutations, , London: Routledge and Kegan Paul; Rittel, H.W.J., Webber, M.M., Dilemmas in a general theory of planning (1973) Policy Sciences, 4 (4), pp. 155-169. , http://www.metu.edu.tr/~baykan/arch467/Rittel%2BWebber%2BDilemmas.pdf, Retrieved October 31, 2011, from; Schön, D., (1983) The reflective practitioner, , London: Basic Books; Schön, D.A., Rein, M., (1994) Frame reflection: Toward the resolution of intractable policy controversies, , New York: Basic Books; Shaw, I., Crompton, A., Theory like mist on spectacles obscures vision (2003) Evaluation, 9 (2), pp. 192-204; Sibeon, R., (2007) Contemporary sociology and policy analysis, , Eastham,UK: Tudor Business Publishing; Tight, M., Research into higher education: An a-theoretical community of practice? (2004) Higher Education Research & Development, 23 (4), pp. 395-411; Tooley, J., Darby, D., (1998) Educational research: A critique. A survey of published educational research, , London: OFSTED; Trowler, P., Captured by the discourse? The socially constitutive power of new higher education discourse in the UK (2001) Organization, 8 (2), pp. 183-201; Webb, A., Co-ordination: A problem in public sector management (1991) Policy and Politics, 19 (4), pp. 221-241; Yin, R., (2008) Case study research: Design and methods, , London: Sage},
  abstract = {'Wicked' issues are one-off problems that have no algorithms to follow to solutions. They are ill-understood or understood in multiple, perhaps conflicting, ways and are fundamentally complex in character. This paper argues that the term is an apt one to describe many aspects of the relationship between theory and data in educational research, and the decisions that have to be made about theory and its role in relation to data. The paper explores these aspects, focusing on the theoretical choices researchers often need to make. It argues that while there is great benefit in making good, explicit, theoretical choices, there are several traps that lie in wait for the unwary and fundamental decisions about the role of theory in research. In clarifying the dimensions of wickedity in this regard, this paper offers assistance to researchers in thinking through the issues they face. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
  langid = {english},
  keywords = {/unread,data,research,theory,wickedity}
}

