  abstract = {\# Background Self-citation appears to be widely prevalent. However, the structural drivers of self-citation are poorly understood. \# Methods Data for this study were obtained from a recently published study of Scopus data aggregated across all authors with \textbackslash{$>$}5 publications, across all scientific fields, which yielded aggregate, country-level data on the mean co-author self-citation rate for the period 1960-2018. These data were merged with 2018 data from Transparency International on corruption, and additional data extracted from the World Development Indicators. The country-level association between the self-citation rate and the corruption index was estimated using multivariable linear regression. \# Results Across 178 countries, the correlation between the mean self-citation rate and the corruption index was -0.52, 95\% confidence interval},
