00992776; Klompien, K., The writer and the text: Basic writers, research papers and plagiarism, , Paper presented at the Annual meeting of the Conference on College Composition and Communication, Denver, March; Lave, J., Wenger, E., (1991) Situated learning: Legitimate peripheral participation, , Cambridge, Cambridge,: Cambridge University Press; Lea, M., Street, B., Student writing in higher education: An academic literacies approach (1998) Studies in Higher Education, 23 (2), pp. 157-172. , doi:10.1080/03075079812331380364; Metcalf, M., (2006) Reading critically at university, , London, London,: Sage; (2010) Citing and referencing, , http://www.lib.monash.edu.au/tutorials/citing/, Monash University, Retrieved from; Morris, I., (1999) My idea; your idea? A study of the attribution practices of second year, Management Major, Bachelor or Business students, , (Unpublished Masters thesis, University of Western Australia, Perth); Nickerson, R.S., The teaching of thinking and problem solving (1994) Thinking and problem solving, pp. 409-450. , In: Sternberg R. J., editors 2, San Diego, CA, San Diego, CA,: Academic Press; Park, C., In other (people's) words: Plagiarism by university students - literature and lessons (2003) Assessment and Evaluation in Higher Education, 28 (5), pp. 471-488. , doi:10.1080/02602930301677; Paul, R., (1992) Critical thinking: What every person needs to survive in a rapidly changing world (Rev. 2nd ed.), , Santa Rosa, CA, Santa Rosa, CA,: The Foundation for Critical Thinking; Pennycook, A., Borrowing other's words: Text, ownership, memory and plagiarism (1996) TESOL Quarterly, 30 (2), pp. 201-230. , doi:10.2307/3588141; Pittam, G., Elander, J., Lusher, J., Fox, P., Payne, N., Student beliefs and attitudes about authorial identity in academic writing (2009) Studies in Higher Education, 34 (2), pp. 153-170. , doi:10.1080/03075070802528270; Price, M., Beyond 'gotcha': Situating plagiarism in policy and pedagogy (2002) College Composition and Communication, 54 (1), pp. 88-116. , doi:10.2307/1512103; (2010) Purdue online writing lab: Using research, , http://owl.english.purdue.edu/owl/section/2/9/, Purdue University, Retrieved September 20, 2010, from; Roy, A., From practice to theory: Writing across the disciplines at Spelman College (1999) Writing, teaching and learning in the disciplines, pp. 119-131. , In: Herrington A., Moran C., editors New York, New York,: Harper & Row; Scollon, R., Plagiarism and ideology (1995) Language in Society, 24 (1), pp. 1-28. , doi:10.1017/S0047404500018388; Scriven, M., Paul, R., (1996) Defining critical thinking, , http://www.sonoma.edu/cthink/university/univclass/defining.nclk, Retrieved from; Swales, J., Citation analysis and discourse analysis (1986) Applied Linguistics, 7 (1), pp. 39-56. , doi:10.1093/applin/7.1.39; Valentine, K., Plagiarism as literacy practice: Recognising and rethinking ethical binaries (2006) College Composition and Communication, 58 (1), pp. 89-110},
  abstract = {Just as plagiarism is viewed poorly in the academic community, so is plagiarism viewed poorly in student writing, with a range of sanctions and penalties applying for not displaying academic integrity. Yet learning to cite effectively to progress one's argument, position or understandings is a skill that takes time to develop and hone. This paper examines the skills underlying effective use of citation and the problems students have in citing effectively. It compares these to the messages that beginning students receive about citation through a focus on academic integrity, plagiarism and punishment. It examines the prevalence of plagiarism and the development of citation skills in student writing when the teaching focuses instead on developing critical writing abilities through the skillful selection, use and acknowledgement of sources. \copyright{} 2012 Copyright HERDSA.},
